message.content:
{
  "157": {
    "document_id": "157",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The document is only a bare title/date line ('Chair Small Business Committee on Mark Up on Venture Capital and Education bills') with no substantive content. There is no claim of personal responsibility for any delivered government benefit, so there is nothing to classify as credit-claiming."
  },
  "189": {
    "document_id": "189",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Leahy's hearing statement advocates for cameras in federal courtrooms and criticizes the Bush administration's secrecy. He mentions cosponsoring the Sunshine in the Courtroom Act and similar bills, but these are pending, unpassed measures. Under negative clarification (2), cosponsorship of unpassed legislation presented as advocacy is not credit-claiming, and the statement is otherwise pure position-taking (negative clarification 1)."
  },
  "744": {
    "document_id": "744",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Allard sent a letter to the Acting Secretary of the Air Force requesting that the Air Force establish clear religious expression guidelines at the Academy. This is a request urging future action that has not yet occurred, which negative clarification (3) excludes from credit-claiming. No benefit has been delivered."
  },
  "873": {
    "document_id": "873",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces a favorable agency decision (Medicare penalty waiver and enrollment extension for hurricane-affected seniors) and explicitly frames it as a response to Landrieu's intervention: 'Administrator finally responds to Sen. Landrieu's letter,' and Landrieu says the agency 'recognized the inconsistency our delegation brought to its attention.' Under clarification (3), claiming that the senator's letter/pressure caused a favorable executive decision (a waiver) is credit-claiming."
  },
  "964": {
    "document_id": "964",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Landrieu and Kerry 'call for' local contracts in Katrina/Rita rebuilding and write the SBA to speed up loss verification. These are demands and letters urging future action that has not yet occurred, excluded by negative clarification (3). No delivered benefit is claimed."
  },
  "1043": {
    "document_id": "1043",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces a concrete grant ($78,046) to the Troy Township Fire Department and ties it directly to DeWine's actions: he 'wrote and passed the FIRE Act,' and 'helped secure $650 million for the FIRE Act.' This matches clarification (1) (announcement of federal grants benefiting the state with verbs like 'secured') and clarification (2) (authorship of enacted legislation). Clear credit-claiming."
  },
  "1139": {
    "document_id": "1139",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "This is a condolence statement on the death of Rep. Charlie Norwood. Negative clarification (4) excludes commemorations and condolences. 'I was proud to work with Rep. Norwood' is a passing mention of past cooperation without claiming causation of any delivered benefit."
  },
  "1610": {
    "document_id": "1610",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Reid's statement at the CHIP enrollment ceremony urges the President not to veto the bill and explicitly credits others ('Chairman Baucus and Senator Rockefeller have worked tirelessly with Senators Grassley and Hatch'). This is position-taking and pressure on the President regarding pending action, plus crediting colleagues rather than himself (negative clarifications 1, 3, and 5). Reid claims no personal causal role in a delivered benefit."
  },
  "1736": {
    "document_id": "1736",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "The release centers on Domenici requesting an expedited hearing and markup for the unpassed Livestock Assistance Act (S.165) and letters urging committee action - requests for future action excluded by negative clarification (3), and cosponsorship of unpassed legislation excluded by negative clarification (2). The final paragraph notes that Domenici and others had 'asked' USDA and the President to approve emergency aid and that USDA later announced emergency loans, but it does not explicitly claim their action caused the loan announcement (no 'secured' or equivalent causal language), so I treated it as context rather than a causal credit claim. This was the main ambiguity; the dominant purpose of the release is advocacy for pending action."
  },
  "2368": {
    "document_id": "2368",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Craig 'announced' a concrete benefit for Idaho constituents - expanded VA clinic services in Lewiston (five days per week plus mental health services) - and links it to his own advocacy: he thanks VA officials for their 'willingness to hear my concerns and - more importantly - respond to the needs of the veterans.' This fits clarification (1) (announcement of a facility/service benefiting the state) and clarification (3) (the senator's appeal causing a favorable agency decision)."
  },
  "2382": {
    "document_id": "2382",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "While most of the release introduces the unpassed CARES Act (advocacy that alone would not count under negative clarification 2), the final paragraph states: 'By intervening with the Department of Homeland Security and removing bureaucratic red tape, Sen. Landrieu recently ensured that Rayan, a little girl from Iraq... was able to come... to the United States for treatment.' This is an explicit claim that the senator's intervention caused a favorable executive outcome, squarely within clarification (3)."
  },
  "2697": {
    "document_id": "2697",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Inouye announces $225,000 in federal funding to control coqui frogs on the Big Island and explicitly claims personal causation: after OMB denied the request, 'I then went looking and found another federal funding source to at least begin the control and containment process.' This is a textbook announcement of federal funds secured through the senator's own efforts (clarification 1 and 3)."
  },
  "2827": {
    "document_id": "2827",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "This is an op-ed by Sen. Lieberman about the war on terrorism and Iraq policy - pure position-taking, argument, and praise of President Bush ('It is to the credit of President Bush'). Under negative clarifications (1) and (5), this is not credit-claiming; no government benefit is claimed as the senator's own achievement."
  },
  "2895": {
    "document_id": "2895",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release reports Senate passage of the Improving America's Security Act and states 'The bill also contains McCaskill's amendment to provide whistleblower protection and collective bargaining rights for airport screeners.' An amendment included in a bill that has passed the chamber meets clarification (2) (a provision included in a package that advanced beyond introduction). The framing of Missouri receiving homeland security funds reinforces the delivered-benefit claim."
  },
  "3038": {
    "document_id": "3038",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Snowe's statement supports Vice Admiral Allen's nomination as Coast Guard Commandant, praising his record and the Coast Guard generally. This is pure position-taking and praise of others (negative clarification 1). She claims no personal responsibility for any government benefit, funding, or decision; there is no causal claim linking her actions to a delivered outcome."
  },
  "3143": {
    "document_id": "3143",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release reports Senate passage (79-14) of the Farm Bill and states 'Graham worked to include additional grant programs that could benefit research and training being conducted in South Carolina' and that the bill 'includes a Graham backed provision that would provide economic adjustment assistance... to textile manufacturers.' Provisions the senator worked to include in a chamber-passed bill meet clarification (2) for credit-claiming (authorship/leadership of provisions in a package that advanced beyond introduction)."
  },
  "3149": {
    "document_id": "3149",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release reports Senate passage of the FY2008 budget resolution and lists among its components 'A provision championed by Reed to help establish an affordable housing fund.' Claiming that a provision the senator championed was included in a chamber-passed package fits clarification (2) (a provision included in a package that advanced beyond introduction). While Reed's statement is partly general praise of the budget, the explicit 'championed by Reed' claim for a provision in the passed resolution constitutes credit-claiming."
  },
  "3817": {
    "document_id": "3817",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Sessions comments on the TVA Board's decision to proceed with a nuclear license application, saying he is 'pleased' and 'I applaud the Board.' Under negative clarification (6), welcoming or applauding decisions made by others without asserting a causal role is not credit-claiming. His statement that Bellefonte 'has been a priority of mine since I came to the Senate' is a passing mention of past support without claiming he caused the TVA decision, which the codebook says is insufficient."
  },
  "4328": {
    "document_id": "4328",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Alexander and Corker announce a favorable executive decision - DOT support for nonstop Jackson-Cincinnati air service under the Essential Air Service program - and state that they 'wrote a letter in support of Jackson's application to Department of Transportation Secretary Mary Peters.' This fits clarification (3): the senators' letter/appeal linked to a favorable agency decision benefiting their state, presented as an announcement of a delivered benefit (clarification 1). It goes beyond merely welcoming the decision because their supportive intervention is asserted."
  },
  "4403": {
    "document_id": "4403",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the release primarily introduces the unpassed R-HoPE Act (which alone would be advocacy under negative clarification 2), it also claims a past legislative achievement: the bill extends provisions of the enacted 2003 Medicare Modernization Act 'that Domenici first authored and offered as legislation in February 2003' to raise Medicare reimbursement for rural physicians. Under clarification (2), claims of authorship of legislation that was enacted count as credit-claiming, and the codebook states claims about past achievements count equally. The ambiguity is that the release's main purpose is the new bill, but the explicit authorship claim for enacted provisions tips it to credit-claiming."
  }
}

{
  "133": {
    "document_id": "133",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kerry criticizes the administration's Dubai Ports deal in light of the parent company's participation in the Israel boycott and demands congressional review. This is position-taking and criticism of the administration with no claim of responsibility for a delivered benefit, per negative clarification (1)."
  },
  "214": {
    "document_id": "214",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "A Pearl Harbor remembrance honoring servicemembers. This is a commemoration with no claim of responsibility for any government action or benefit, squarely within negative clarification (4) (commemorations)."
  },
  "299": {
    "document_id": "299",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Alexander receives a 'Hero of the Taxpayer' award from Americans for Tax Reform based on his voting record. This is an award presentation plus an explanation of his votes ('I was proud to side with taxpayers on key votes'), both excluded by negative clarifications (4) and (1); he claims no delivered benefit."
  },
  "490": {
    "document_id": "490",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Webb responds to the NIE on Iran by criticizing the Bush administration's refusal to engage adversaries. This is foreign-policy position-taking and criticism with no claim of causing any government outcome, per negative clarification (1)."
  },
  "630": {
    "document_id": "630",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dodd comments on Bush's proposed budget, praising Connecticut-relevant defense spending and urging two additional submarines per year. This is advocacy/position-taking on a pending budget and a request for future action, not a claim to have delivered a benefit, per negative clarifications (1) and (3)."
  },
  "721": {
    "document_id": "721",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Reid salutes Iraqi elections and troops but criticizes the administration for lacking a plan and vows Democrats will keep demanding a strategy. This is position-taking and criticism of the administration without any causal claim to a government benefit, per negative clarification (1)."
  },
  "773": {
    "document_id": "773",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "McCaskill and colleagues passed a resolution honoring the late Senator Eagleton. This is a commemorative/honorary resolution, excluded under negative clarification (4) (commemorations and condolences); no concrete benefit to constituents is claimed."
  },
  "1060": {
    "document_id": "1060",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release emphasizes that 'Nelson's Small Business Health Care Amendment' was included in the SCHIP reauthorization that the Senate passed. Under clarification (2), a claim of sponsorship/authorship of a provision or amendment included in legislation that has advanced beyond introduction (here, Senate passage) is credit-claiming, and the release frames the amendment's inclusion as Nelson's contribution."
  },
  "1086": {
    "document_id": "1086",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dayton 'applauds' Senate passage of $1 billion in LIHEAP funding while lamenting it is insufficient; his own four amendments to fully fund the program were defeated. Applauding an outcome produced by others without asserting a causal role falls under negative clarification (6), and his own efforts did not deliver a benefit, so there is no credit-claim."
  },
  "1155": {
    "document_id": "1155",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although much of the statement urges future work on an asbestos trust fund (position-taking), Leahy invokes a concrete past legislative achievement: 'With the unanimous adoption of the Leahy Hatch medical criteria amendment this committee reached consensus on the proper standards.' Clarification (2) treats an adopted amendment bearing the senator's authorship as credit-claiming, and clarification (4) says claims about past achievements count equally. The ambiguity is that the claim is embedded in a larger advocacy statement, but the named, adopted amendment satisfies the codebook's criterion."
  },
  "1313": {
    "document_id": "1313",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Lieberman expresses frustration that the House will not take up the D.C. voting rights bill, commends the efforts of Davis, Norton, Hatch, Bennett, and Utah officials, and hopes for future action. No benefit was delivered, and he credits others rather than claiming a causal role in any achieved outcome; this is position-taking per negative clarification (1) and (5)."
  },
  "1827": {
    "document_id": "1827",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The headline and text state that provisions of 'Sen. Dick Lugar's Good Samaritan Hunger Relief Act' will be included in the Grassley-Baucus Katrina tax relief package. Under clarification (2), claims of authorship of a provision included in a legislative package (advanced beyond introduction) constitute credit-claiming; the release presents the inclusion of Lugar's authored provision as his achievement."
  },
  "2003": {
    "document_id": "2003",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Clinton's office announces that the Air Force will place a new Predator squadron in New York, framing it as good news and economic benefit for the state. Clarification (1) explicitly includes announcements of facilities/projects benefiting the senator's state, 'especially with verbs like... announced.' I note some tension with negative clarification (6) since she 'hailed' the decision without explicitly claiming she secured it, but the release is structured as her announcement of a delivered federal benefit, which the codebook treats as credit-claiming."
  },
  "2143": {
    "document_id": "2143",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Murray's statement mourns the passing of former state party chair Karen Marchioro. This is a condolence/memorial statement, explicitly excluded by negative clarification (4), with no claim of responsibility for any government outcome."
  },
  "3047": {
    "document_id": "3047",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces USDA's purchase of up to 80 million pounds of potatoes and explicitly links it to the Idaho Congressional Delegation's March 15 letter requesting the action. This matches clarification (3) (a senator's letter causing a favorable agency decision, including purchases) and clarification (4) (joint delegation claims count)."
  },
  "3201": {
    "document_id": "3201",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Talent announces USDA's disaster designation for Missouri and asserts a personal causal role: he 'worked with USDA officials to expedite the approval of the Governor's request.' This fits clarification (1) (announcing a disaster declaration benefiting the state) and clarification (3) (claiming his intervention helped cause a favorable executive decision); the earlier ECP funds announcement reinforces the credit claim."
  },
  "3914": {
    "document_id": "3914",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Frist credits 'Republican economic policies' and the Bush tax cuts for job growth while attacking Democrats. Crediting an entire party's policies rather than the senator personally is excluded by negative clarification (5), and the statement is general position-taking with no claim of a specific delivered benefit."
  },
  "3950": {
    "document_id": "3950",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Harkin expresses hope that the new Finley diabetes facility will help Iowans but does not claim to have secured it; the release also notes he authored the HeLP America Act, which is unpassed legislation offered as advocacy. Under negative clarifications (2) and (6), there is no causal claim of a delivered benefit."
  },
  "4124": {
    "document_id": "4124",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kennedy commends President Bush's call for comprehensive immigration reform and urges turning 'talk into action.' This is praise of another actor plus advocacy for pending/future legislation, excluded by negative clarifications (1), (3), and (6); Kennedy claims no delivered outcome."
  },
  "4344": {
    "document_id": "4344",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Salazar announces he is joining a bipartisan group supporting the Senate-White House PATRIOT Act compromise and praises its provisions. This is support for pending policy without any claim of authorship or causal responsibility for the compromise's contents, per negative clarification (1); 'I am pleased the compromise includes...' expresses approval, not credit."
  }
}

{  "42": {
    "document_id": "42",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Lott and Collins announce that their negotiated agreement 'resulted in the following provisions being included in the Collins Lieberman legislation,' an explicit causal claim that their intervention shaped a legislative package (FEMA made independent within DHS, direct access to the President, etc.). Under clarification (2), claims of leadership for 'a provision included in a package' count as credit-claiming even before final passage. I acknowledge ambiguity: the underlying bill is only being introduced, which echoes negative clarification (2) on unpassed legislation, but this is not mere advocacy of one's own bill—it is a claim that their compromise produced concrete provisions in someone else's legislative vehicle, going beyond introduction-stage advocacy."
  },
  "714": {
    "document_id": "714",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although much of the column is informational about the U.S. Marshals Service, the senator explicitly states, 'I helped secure more than $3 billion in the fiscal year 2006 Homeland Security appropriations bill for ICE's domestic enforcement efforts, as well as funding for 250 additional criminal investigators.' This is a textbook 'helped secure' funding claim under clarification (1), making the document credit-claiming despite its ceremonial/informational framing."
  },
  "794": {
    "document_id": "794",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Amid considerable position-taking (Iraq, intelligence, taxes), Bond claims 'The new federal highway bill, which Bond co-authored, is providing Missouri with much-needed resources, including $1 billion in new money — more than $4 billion in total funding.' This asserts co-authorship of enacted legislation currently delivering concrete funds to his state, matching clarification (2) (authorship of legislation that has been enacted) and the definition's causal link between his actions and a delivered government benefit."
  },
  "805": {
    "document_id": "805",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Shelby, identified as a member of the Appropriations Committee, 'announced that the Department of Homeland Security will release $3,510 to the Homewood Fire Department' through a federal grant program. Clarification (1) covers announcements of federal grants benefiting the senator's constituents, 'especially with verbs like ... \"announced.\"' Even without an explicit 'I secured,' the senator's announcement of federal funds flowing to his state is credit-claiming under the codebook."
  },
  "1257": {
    "document_id": "1257",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The headline itself ('PARMA RECEIVES FIREFIGHTING GRANT THROUGH SEN. DEWINE'S BILL') ties a local grant to DeWine. The release states DeWine 'wrote and passed the FIRE Act,' 'helped secure approximately $650 million' for it, and announces $105,550 to the Parma Fire Department. This combines clarification (1) (announced grant to state constituents, 'helped secure' funds) and clarification (2) (authorship of enacted legislation)—an unambiguous credit claim."
  },
  "1325": {
    "document_id": "1325",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Dole's floor statement argues that the judicial nominations process has broken down, criticizes Democrats' filibusters, and expresses support for Senator Frist's approach. It is pure position-taking and criticism of other actors with no claim of responsibility for any delivered benefit, squarely within negative clarification (1)."
  },
  "1749": {
    "document_id": "1749",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Hutchison 'signed on as an original cosponsor' of the Veterans Housing Benefits Enhancement Act, which was 'introduced today.' She expresses support and intent to work for passage, but claims no delivered benefit. This is cosponsorship of unpassed legislation presented as advocacy, excluded by negative clarification (2)."
  },
  "1897": {
    "document_id": "1897",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Martinez states he cannot support the pending Pombo OCS bill in its current form, praises Congressman Putnam, and threatens to join a filibuster unless the buffer is extended. This is a statement of position on pending policy with no claim of having caused any government outcome—negative clarification (1) (support/opposition for pending policy)."
  },
  "1927": {
    "document_id": "1927",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Snowe 'announced that $8.1 million has been included in the FY 2006 Military Construction, Veterans Affairs Appropriations bill for the Portsmouth Naval Shipyard. This funding was requested by Snowe and Sen. Susan Collins,' and the bill 'passed the Senate today.' She also highlights veterans funding she requested via letter to appropriators. This matches clarification (1) (announced appropriation secured for the state, joint delegation claims count per clarification (4)) and clarification (2) (requested provision included in a bill that advanced)."
  },
  "2335": {
    "document_id": "2335",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release is primarily an essay contest announcement, which negative clarification (4) expressly excludes ('essay contests'). However, it also asserts that Bayh's Responsible Fatherhood Act had 'parts ... signed into the law by President Bush earlier this year' and that as governor he 'more than doubled the amount of money collected for child support payments'—claims of authorship of enacted legislation (clarification (2)) and past concrete achievements, which clarification (4) says 'count equally.' Following the codebook's Durbin positive example, where embedded past credit claims sufficed despite a non-credit primary purpose, I classify this as credit-claiming, though I note the tension with the essay-contest exclusion."
  },
  "2869": {
    "document_id": "2869",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Jeffords simply explains why he voted for the Kerry and Levin amendments on Iraq ('I voted in support ... because our nation needs a plan'). Negative clarification (1) expressly excludes 'explanations of votes' and pure position-taking; there is no claim of responsibility for any delivered outcome."
  },
  "3252": {
    "document_id": "3252",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Nelson announces that 'his amendment ... passed on a bipartisan vote of 72 to 26,' calling it 'a victory for Nebraska's ranchers.' An adopted amendment is expressly listed in clarification (2) as credit-claiming ('an amendment adopted'), and he claims personal authorship ('The Nelson amendment') of the provision benefiting his state's cattle producers."
  },
  "4022": {
    "document_id": "4022",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kennedy's floor statement introduces 'Medicare for All' legislation and argues at length for its merits while criticizing the administration. It is advocacy of unpassed legislation and position-taking, excluded by negative clarifications (1) and (2); no benefit is claimed to have been delivered."
  },
  "4122": {
    "document_id": "4122",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Beyond announcing a hearing, the release claims the senators' subcommittee oversight produced results: 'Due to the Subcommittee's oversight, collections from delinquent Defense contractors skyrocketed by 4,415% ... to $31 million in 2006,' and levies 'raking in an additional $122 million in back taxes.' Clarification (3) expressly includes claims that the senator's 'oversight caused a favorable executive or agency decision (... recovered funds).' The causal claim linking their investigation to recovered taxpayer funds is credit-claiming."
  },
  "4184": {
    "document_id": "4184",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Kyl 'co-sponsored legislation that calls for 2,000 additional front-line border patrol positions'; his amendment to the Budget Resolution 'calls for' $352 million. No adoption or passage is claimed—it is sponsorship/advocacy of unpassed legislation, excluded by negative clarification (2)."
  },
  "4207": {
    "document_id": "4207",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The headline action—a letter asking Secretary Chertoff to consider a levee proposal—is a request for future action excluded by negative clarification (3). However, the release also states Cornyn 'added an amendment to the Department of State and Foreign Operations Appropriations bill that required the IBWC ... to report to Congress,' claiming an adopted amendment/provision in an appropriations bill, which clarification (2) treats as credit-claiming. This mirrors the codebook's Durbin positive example, where a letter urging action was classified as credit-claiming because of embedded claims of past accomplishments. I note some ambiguity since the dominant purpose is the prospective request."
  },
  "4295": {
    "document_id": "4295",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "Although the immediate news is the introduction of a new FMAP bill (advocacy of unpassed legislation, negative clarification (2)), the release claims that in 2003 Murkowski 'helped win a two-year change in the Economic Growth Reconciliation Act ... that provided the state with an additional $34.11 million above the traditional Medicaid formula amount.' That is a causal claim of having delivered concrete federal funds to Alaska, covered by clarification (1) ('helped win' funding) and clarification (4) (past achievements count equally)."
  },
  "4508": {
    "document_id": "4508",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "The release announces FEMA moving trailers and mobile homes to Mississippi and states: 'The announcement follows the Senator's Monday appeal to President Bush to authorize the immediate transport of available trailers.' This construction asserts that Lott's personal appeal caused the favorable executive action, exactly what clarification (3) covers ('claims that the senator's ... appeal ... caused a favorable executive or agency decision'). Although Lott also thanks the President and FEMA, the press release asserts his own causal role, distinguishing it from merely 'welcoming' another's decision (negative clarification (6))."
  },
  "4677": {
    "document_id": "4677",
    "label": "0",
    "explanation": "Hutchison questioned officials at a hearing, 'expressed her support' for the budget request, and said 'I have supported increasing the Army's end strength and am encouraged to see $18 million in the emergency supplemental for facilities at Fort Hood.' This is precisely the 'passing mention of past support (\"I have supported...\") without claiming causation of a benefit' that the negative clarification's final sentence deems insufficient; she does not claim to have secured the $18 million."
  },
  "4774": {
    "document_id": "4774",
    "label": "1",
    "explanation": "While the release urges conferees to retain the provision (a future-oriented request), its central fact is that 'the Senate unanimously approved an amendment offered by Senators Bayh and Richard Durbin' to end the Patriot Penalty. Claiming authorship of an amendment the Senate adopted falls squarely within clarification (2) ('an amendment adopted ... chamber passage'), and joint claims with colleagues count per clarification (4)."
  }
}
